TASK W2-04 — run the pipeline as a subprocess (log, warn, map the exit) and the fake pipeline every later row tests against
Repo /home/dev/work/dippe/dp-forge · base f28009aa · package tools/dpf (not a workspace: run every command from the repo root)
You write the failing tests AND the implementation. Touch only the files under FILES.

WHY
dpf drives spec-pipeline as a subprocess (A1): it spawns it, appends its output to a log,
surfaces its WARNING lines, and maps its exit code to a run result. Without the runner,
every screen and the flow (W2-13) has nothing to call; without the fake, none of the later
rows can test a pipeline without the GPU or the model server. Row W2-13 (not yours) uses
the runner in the flow.

FILES
tools/dpf/src/run/runner.ts                     new
tools/dpf/tests/run/runner.test.ts              new
tools/dpf/tests/fixtures/fake-spec-pipeline.mjs new

SITES (verified at f28009aa; if a line moved, find it again with the grep anchor)
S1 tools/dpf/src/run/runner.ts   new   (C9's RunOptions/RunResult are the contract; EXIT_OK and EXIT_PAUSED come from 'spec-pipeline/protocol', W2-01 — never a literal 0/4, C6)
S2 tools/spec-pipeline/src/driver/exit-codes.ts:7-26   grep 'EXIT_OK'   exit codes 0/2/3/4/5/6/7/8 the runner maps
S3 tools/spec-pipeline/src/evidence/trim.ts:149   grep 'spec-pipeline: WARNING'   the WARNING prefix the pipeline prints; onWarning fires per line starting with it
S4 tools/spec-pipeline/src/ir/command.ts:481   grep 'err: line =>'   the pipeline writes its stderr lines with a trailing newline (safe to line-buffer)
S5 tools/spec-pipeline/src/doctor/command-runner.ts:13-18   grep "child.on('close'"   the repo's spawn pattern: resolve on 'close' (all stdio drained), handle 'error'

PATTERN TO COPY (02-contracts.md C9, verbatim)
export interface RunOptions {
  readonly command: readonly string[]; // default [process.execPath, ...process.execArgv, SPEC_PIPELINE_CLI]; tests pass a fake
  readonly cwd: string;
  readonly args: readonly string[];    // ['run', '--manifest', shippedManifestPath(stage), '--profile', p, '--config', c, '--branch', b, ...]
  readonly logFile: string;            // child stdout and stderr are appended here
  readonly onWarning: (line: string) => void; // each stderr line starting `spec-pipeline: WARNING`
}
export type RunResult = { readonly kind: 'done' } | { readonly kind: 'paused'; readonly code: 4 } | { readonly kind: 'failed'; readonly code: number };
SIGINT is forwarded to the child once; dpf then waits for it.

DELIVERABLES (closed list: do all of them, nothing else)
D1 tools/dpf/src/run/runner.ts: export the RunOptions and RunResult types (C9; RunResult's
   paused code is written `typeof EXIT_PAUSED`, the same literal type 4 without a magic
   number) and runPipeline, exactly this shape (verified by tsc --strict, eslint with the
   repo rules, and a run against a child script: every line reaches the log before 'close',
   a last line without a newline is still delivered, ENOENT resolves instead of hanging):
     import { type ChildProcess, spawn } from 'node:child_process';
     import { createWriteStream, mkdirSync, type WriteStream } from 'node:fs';
     import { dirname } from 'node:path';
     import { createInterface } from 'node:readline';
     import type { Readable } from 'node:stream';

     import { EXIT_OK, EXIT_PAUSED } from 'spec-pipeline/protocol';

     const WARNING_PREFIX = 'spec-pipeline: WARNING';
     /** A child killed by a signal has no exit code: the shell convention for SIGINT. */
     const SIGNAL_EXIT = 130;
     /** The command could not start (ENOENT, EACCES): no pipeline ran. */
     const SPAWN_FAILED_EXIT = 1;

     const resultOf = (code: number | null): RunResult => {
       if (code === EXIT_OK) return { kind: 'done' };
       if (code === EXIT_PAUSED) return { kind: 'paused', code: EXIT_PAUSED };
       return { kind: 'failed', code: code ?? SIGNAL_EXIT };
     };

     const watchStderr = (stderr: Readable, log: WriteStream, onWarning: (line: string) => void): void => {
       createInterface({ input: stderr, crlfDelay: Infinity }).on('line', line => {
         log.write(`${line}\n`);
         if (line.startsWith(WARNING_PREFIX)) onWarning(line);
       });
     };

     const openLog = (file: string): WriteStream => {
       mkdirSync(dirname(file), { recursive: true });
       return createWriteStream(file, { flags: 'a' });
     };

     /** Settles once the child is gone AND the log is flushed; the SIGINT forwarder goes with it. */
     const awaitChild = (child: ChildProcess, log: WriteStream): Promise<RunResult> =>
       new Promise(resolve => {
         const forward = (): void => {
           child.kill('SIGINT');
         };
         const settle = (result: RunResult): void => {
           process.removeListener('SIGINT', forward);
           log.end(() => {
             resolve(result);
           });
         };
         process.once('SIGINT', forward);
         child.on('error', () => {
           settle({ kind: 'failed', code: SPAWN_FAILED_EXIT });
         });
         child.on('close', code => {
           settle(resultOf(code));
         });
       });

     export const runPipeline = (o: RunOptions): Promise<RunResult> => {
       const log = openLog(o.logFile);
       const [bin, ...rest] = o.command;
       const child = spawn(bin, [...rest, ...o.args], { cwd: o.cwd });
       child.stdout.pipe(log, { end: false });
       watchStderr(child.stderr, log, o.onWarning);
       return awaitChild(child, log);
     };
   'close' (not 'exit') is the resolve point: it fires after stdout/stderr are drained, and
   log.end's callback waits for the log to be flushed, so a test can read the log right after
   the await. The SIGINT listener is removed on settle: a once-listener left behind would leak
   one listener per stage run and forward a later Ctrl-C to a dead child.
   If `bin` types as `string | undefined` under your tsconfig, STOP (BRIEF MISMATCH): the
   root tsconfig.scripts.json has no noUncheckedIndexedAccess at f28009aa.
D2 tools/dpf/tests/fixtures/fake-spec-pipeline.mjs: a node ESM script (no imports other than
   node:fs, node:path). On each invocation:
     1. plan = JSON.parse(readFileSync(process.env.FAKE_SP_PLAN)); counterFile = FAKE_SP_PLAN + '.counter';
        n = the number in counterFile, or 0 if it is absent; entry = plan[n]; write n + 1 to counterFile.
        (The FIRST call takes plan[0].)
     2. mkdir -p FAKE_SP_DIR and FAKE_SP_DIR/paused.
     3. entry.ledger (an array, optional): append each element to FAKE_SP_DIR/ledger.jsonl as one
        JSON line. entry.state (optional): replace FAKE_SP_DIR/state.json with
        JSON.stringify(entry.state, null, 2) + '\n'. entry.paused ({step, payload}, optional):
        replace FAKE_SP_DIR/paused/<step>.json with JSON.stringify(entry.paused.payload, null, 2) + '\n'.
        These are the pipeline dir's real names (LEDGER_FILENAME, STATE_FILENAME, PAUSE_DIRNAME);
        the fake is plain node and cannot import the .ts protocol, so it spells them.
     4. append JSON.stringify(process.argv.slice(2)) + '\n' to calls.jsonl beside FAKE_SP_PLAN
        (slice(2): argv[0] is node, argv[1] is this script — W2-05's T5 indexes argv from 'ir').
     5. write entry.stderr (an array of lines, optional) to stderr, each + '\n', and
        entry.stdout (an array of lines, optional) to stdout, each + '\n' (W2-05's ir diff
        lines and ir findings come back on stdout).
     6. process.exit(entry.exit).
   Keep it declarative (forEach, no for/let): eslint lints tools/dpf/**/*.mjs too (W2-01b D3).

